  "overview": {
    "short": "Lonar Crater Lake in Buldhana district, Maharashtra, is a world-famous saline soda lake created by a meteorite impact in basalt rock over 50,000 years ago. A declared National Geo-Heritage Monument, it is encircled by 11th-century Hemadpanthi temples and lush forested cliffs.",
    "description": "Lonar Crater Lake is a world-renowned National Geo-Heritage Monument located at Lonar in Buldhana district, Maharashtra. Created approximately 52,000 years ago by a hyper-velocity meteorite impact in basalt rock, it is the only known hyper-velocity impact crater in basalt anywhere on Earth. Spanning an almost circular diameter of 1.8 kilometres with steep 137-metre basalt rim cliffs, the emerald saline-alkaline lake is fringed by unique biodiversity, perennial mineral springs at Gomukh, and magnificent 11th-century Hemadpanthi stone temples including the Daitya Sudhan Temple.",
